00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.160
most of the products are household products but&nbsp;
there are some beauty products in there as well

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.240
this isn't sponsored zero co offered to send me&nbsp;
products after i've reached out to them about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.280
a question but i wanted to try them out over a&nbsp;
longer period of time without too much pressure

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.600
so the concept is zero co is a household&nbsp;
and personal care product company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.560
focuses on reducing single-use plastic and&nbsp;
i'm usually quite skeptical when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.960
to brands that focus on reducing single-use&nbsp;
plastic part of this is because i'm used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.360
reviewing skin care and compared to other&nbsp;
sources of single-use plastic in our lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.600
skincare is really just not that much it&nbsp;
takes a while to finish up most products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.760
but a lot of skincare brands act like you're&nbsp;
going to save the world if you buy their serum

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.560
another big reason is that a lot of plastic free&nbsp;
brands make a lot of trade-offs that don't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.960
make a lot of sense they aren't really well&nbsp;
thought out and it ends up being greenwashing

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.360
for example sometimes they switch to really heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.800
glass containers that end up having&nbsp;
a huge carbon cost for transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.120
and they also break a lot in transport which&nbsp;
means you end up wasting a lot of product

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.280
sometimes brands switch to bioplastics&nbsp;
that might be compostable but not truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.520
biodegradable they can't be recycled and&nbsp;
most people don't compost them properly so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.640
they might have been better off&nbsp;
just using recyclable plastic

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.520
some of these bioplastics also&nbsp;
have a bigger carbon footprint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.720
than traditional plastics if you&nbsp;
make them using renewable energy

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.080
and the final reason is that most eco-friendly&nbsp;
brands are also really into that whole clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.080
beauty scientists-are-trying-to-kill-you&nbsp;
thing i hate this sort of marketing i've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.240
talked about why in my video clean beauty&nbsp;
is a scam and won't give us safer products

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.400
i don't really boycott this because it's&nbsp;
so hard to find brands that have zero clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.760
beauty fear-mongering in their marketing&nbsp;
a lot of the time it's because the brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.840
themselves are misled and i try to be nice to&nbsp;
people who are doing the wrong thing out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.320
good intentions but i do get really annoyed&nbsp;
and if there's an alternative i'll take it

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.000
but zero co do household products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
which is something we use a lot more of so&nbsp;
we have a lot more single-use containers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.040
and australia is pretty bad in terms of&nbsp;
the amount of single-use plastic per person

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.000
zero co also have a lot more dimension than just&nbsp;
taking ocean waste and turning it into bottles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.880
i asked my two favorite sources for&nbsp;
