have come from for example all of mine have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.320
come from indonesia the pouches are also made&nbsp;
from plastic that would have gone to landfill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.720
they can be reused over and over and over for&nbsp;
a very long time according to zero co when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.200
they've worn down they're going to be used to make&nbsp;
something else which zero co haven't disclosed yet

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.560
as well as being reusable these&nbsp;
pouches are a lot lighter than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.160
traditional bottles and so that saves&nbsp;
the carbon emissions from transport

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.960
zero co has also recently introduced carbon&nbsp;
negative deliveries that means that offsetting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.240
two kilograms of carbon dioxide per one kilogram&nbsp;
produced carbon offsetting is a bit of a mixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.040
bag a lot of the time it's used as an excuse&nbsp;
to not actually reduce emissions but i don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.200
think that's what's actually happening here&nbsp;
i think it's just meant to be a nice bonus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.840
i'm sure it's not really a perfect system i&nbsp;
don't think using products is ever going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.320
not using products at all but i prefer not&nbsp;
to live in filth and this seems to be a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.400
better thought out than a lot of systems&nbsp;
i've seen and it covers a lot more bases

